::ENG2_U04_L03_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Research question: How could a school garden support science learning? Source A says gardens let students observe plant growth, soil, and ecosystems. Which note is best?{
~A. Gardens are green sometimes.#Incorrect. This is too vague and not tied to science learning.
=B. Gardens provide observations of plant growth, soil, and ecosystems.#Correct. The note is relevant and concise.
~C. Schools have students.#Incorrect. This does not record useful source evidence.
~D. Ecosystems is a long word.#Incorrect. This does not answer the research question.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Original: A pre-order lunch pilot reduced unwanted trays because staff prepared meals based on student selections. Which paraphrase is accurate?{
~A. Staff ignored student selections.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
~B. Students selected nothing.#Incorrect. The source says meals were based on selections.
~C. The pilot increased unwanted trays.#Incorrect. The source says the pilot reduced them.
=D. The pilot lowered unwanted trays by helping staff prepare meals according to student choices.#Correct. This keeps the source meaning in new wording.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Source A explains that gardens support plant observations. Source B explains that journals help students compare observations. Which answer synthesizes the sources?{
=A. Gardens can provide observations that students use journals to compare and explain.#Correct. This combines the ideas into one explanation.
~B. Source A is first and Source B is second.#Incorrect. This does not combine ideas.
~C. Gardens exist and journals exist.#Incorrect. This is too basic.
~D. Neither source is about science.#Incorrect. Both sources connect to science learning.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which note is least useful for the question, "How could pre-order lunch forms reduce cafeteria food waste?"{
~A. Pre-order forms helped staff prepare meals based on student selections.#Incorrect. This is useful.
~B. The pilot reduced unwanted trays.#Incorrect. This is useful.
=C. The cafeteria walls are painted tan.#Correct. This detail does not help answer the research question.
~D. Reminders helped students complete forms after the first week.#Incorrect. This is useful context.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Original: Student reminders were needed during the first week because some students forgot to complete the form. Which paraphrase is accurate?{
=A. At first, some students needed reminders because they forgot to fill out the form.#Correct. This keeps the meaning and uses different wording.
~B. Students never forgot the form.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
~C. Reminders were never used.#Incorrect. The source says reminders were needed.
~D. Students completed all forms before the pilot began.#Incorrect. The source does not say this.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which statement best explains synthesis?{
~A. Copying one source word for word.#Incorrect. That is copying, not synthesis.
~B. Listing random facts with no connection.#Incorrect. Synthesis explains relationships.
~C. Ignoring all sources.#Incorrect. Synthesis depends on sources.
=D. Combining ideas from multiple sources to explain a larger point.#Correct. Synthesis connects source ideas.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Research question: How could later bus service affect student job access? Which note is most relevant?{
~A. The bus seats are blue.#Incorrect. Seat color does not answer the research question.
=B. Student survey responses show some students cannot reach jobs after school because routes end too early.#Correct. This note directly connects bus service to job access.
~C. A bus stop has a sign.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~D. Buses have wheels.#Incorrect. This is not useful research evidence.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Original: Evening library hours helped students who could not visit before 5:00 p.m. Which paraphrase is best?{
~A. Students could only visit before 5:00 p.m.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
~B. The library closed forever.#Incorrect. This is not stated.
=C. Later library hours gave access to students unavailable before 5:00 p.m.#Correct. This accurately restates the source.
~D. Students never use libraries.#Incorrect. This contradicts the original.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence would work best in an expository paragraph using source notes?{
~A. Lunch forms are just a thing.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~B. Waste is bad, I guess.#Incorrect. This is informal and unsupported.
~C. The source was a source about source things.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
=D. Pre-order forms may reduce waste because they help cafeteria staff prepare meals based on actual student selections.#Correct. This explains source information clearly.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is clearest?{
=A. Students can use garden journals to record observations and connect evidence to science vocabulary.#Correct. This is a complete, clear sentence.
~B. The garden students observations because journals.#Incorrect. This is not a complete clear sentence.
~C. Garden journal science and stuff.#Incorrect. This is informal and incomplete.
~D. Because observations.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which note should be avoided because it copies too much without processing?{
~A. Pre-order pilot reduced unwanted trays.#Incorrect. This is a brief useful note.
~B. Staff used student selections to prepare meals.#Incorrect. This is a concise note.
=C. "A four-week pre-order lunch pilot reduced the number of unwanted trays because cafeteria staff prepared meals based on student selections."#Correct. This copies a full source sentence instead of creating a brief note.
~D. Reminders helped after the first week.#Incorrect. This is a useful short note.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Which paraphrase changes the source meaning? Source: Garden journals help students record observations over time.{
~A. Garden journals can help students keep track of observations across multiple days.#Incorrect. This keeps the meaning.
=B. Garden journals prevent students from making observations.#Correct. This reverses the source meaning, so it is inaccurate.
~C. Students can use journals to record what they observe in the garden.#Incorrect. This keeps the meaning.
~D. Journals support repeated observation records.#Incorrect. This keeps the meaning.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which synthesis best combines these sources? Source A: Compost bins reduced cafeteria trash weight. Source B: Student signs helped students sort food scraps correctly.{
=A. Compost bins reduced trash most effectively when students also had signs that explained how to sort scraps.#Correct. This connects the result from Source A with the support from Source B.
~B. Source A talks about bins. Source B talks about signs.#Incorrect. This lists sources without explaining the relationship.
~C. Compost and signs are different words.#Incorrect. This is not synthesis.
~D. Food scraps do not exist.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source information.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which source note best supports a paragraph about challenges with pre-order lunch forms?{
~A. The pilot reduced unwanted trays.#Incorrect. This supports a benefit.
~B. Meals were prepared from selections.#Incorrect. This explains how the system worked.
~C. The report was about lunch.#Incorrect. This is too general.
=D. Some students forgot to complete the form during the first week.#Correct. This identifies a challenge.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence best uses an appropriate expository voice?{
~A. Lunch forms are totally awesome and nobody can disagree.#Incorrect. This is biased and informal.
~B. The form thing is, like, useful.#Incorrect. This is informal.
=C. Pre-order forms may reduce waste when students complete them consistently.#Correct. This is clear, measured, and explanatory.
~D. Only careless students forget forms.#Incorrect. This is biased and inappropriate.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which action best prepares notes for synthesis?{
~A. Keep only copied paragraphs.#Incorrect. Notes should be brief and organized.
=B. Group notes by idea, such as benefits, challenges, or solutions.#Correct. Grouping notes helps identify connections across sources.
~C. Remove source meanings.#Incorrect. Notes must preserve meaning.
~D. Ignore the research question.#Incorrect. The question guides note selection.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Source: The garden guide says students compare plant changes over several weeks. Which paraphrase is best?{
~A. Plants never change.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
~B. Students only compare rocks.#Incorrect. The source is about plants.
=C. Students look at how plants change across multiple weeks.#Correct. This accurately restates the source.
~D. The guide says nothing about time.#Incorrect. It mentions several weeks.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which note is relevant to the question, "How could later library hours support students?"{
=A. Evening hours helped students who worked after school use library resources.#Correct. This directly connects later hours to student access.
~B. The library has shelves.#Incorrect. This is general and does not answer the question.
~C. The library has a front door.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~D. Books have pages.#Incorrect. This is not useful source evidence.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which synthesis is weakest?{
~A. Sources A and B show that gardens support science learning when observations are recorded and compared.#Incorrect. This is a synthesis.
=B. Source A says one thing. Source B says another thing.#Correct. This is weak because it does not explain the relationship between the sources.
~C. Compost bins reduced trash, and signs helped students use them correctly.#Incorrect. This connects two source ideas.
~D. Later hours improve access when students cannot arrive before closing.#Incorrect. This is an explanatory source-based statement.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which revision fixes the fragment: "Because the report measured waste before and after the pilot."{
~A. Because the report measured waste before and after the pilot, and.#Incorrect. This is still incomplete.
~B. Because measured waste before after pilot.#Incorrect. This is unclear and incomplete.
~C. The report because.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
=D. The report is useful because it measured waste before and after the pilot.#Correct. This is a complete sentence.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] What is the best reason to include a limitation in your notes?{
=A. It helps your research explanation stay accurate and complete.#Correct. Limitations prevent a one-sided or exaggerated explanation.
~B. It means you cannot synthesize.#Incorrect. Limitations can be synthesized with benefits.
~C. It makes the source disappear.#Incorrect. Limitations do not remove a source.
~D. It proves all sources are useless.#Incorrect. A useful source can include limitations.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Which paraphrase is too close to the original? Original: "Staff prepared meals based on student selections."{
~A. The cafeteria used students' choices to decide which meals to make.#Incorrect. This is a good paraphrase.
~B. Student meal choices guided cafeteria preparation.#Incorrect. This restates the idea in new wording.
=C. Staff prepared meals based on student selections.#Correct. This copies the original exactly.
~D. Workers used student orders to plan meals.#Incorrect. This is a paraphrase.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which answer best explains why notes should connect to the research question?{
~A. Notes should include every interesting detail.#Incorrect. Interesting details may not answer the question.
~B. Notes should avoid the source evidence.#Incorrect. Notes should preserve useful evidence.
~C. Research questions are not useful after sources are found.#Incorrect. The question remains important.
=D. Relevant notes help the writer answer the actual inquiry instead of drifting off topic.#Correct. The research question controls note selection.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence best introduces a synthesis in an expository paragraph?{
~A. I don't know but sources are there.#Incorrect. This is informal and unclear.
=B. Two sources suggest that the program works best when the main solution is paired with student support.#Correct. This introduces a connected explanation.
~C. The sources are just two things.#Incorrect. This does not explain the connection.
~D. Everybody should obviously agree.#Incorrect. This is biased.
}

::ENG2_U04_L03_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which checklist best matches Lesson 3 source work?{
=A. Select relevant notes, paraphrase accurately, include limitations, and synthesize sources.#Correct. These are the core Lesson 3 skills.
~B. Copy every sentence, ignore meaning, and avoid synthesis.#Incorrect. This weakens research work.
~C. Use only one source and call it synthesis.#Incorrect. Synthesis combines source ideas.
~D. Choose notes that do not answer the research question.#Incorrect. Notes should be relevant.
}
